# Design: the word lens, the first lens written in form. The page spec/lens.md is the authority; these nodes say what it chose and why.

n: 5.15
name: word-lens
kind: hub
is: The first lens, one module written in form. Words beside the catalogue's numbers and leaves written as people write them turn text into nodes and back; names kept beside a recipe and a let stand for arguments; rows of two shapes turn a surface into the core and back. The canonical print stays what is stored. It is a step toward the home lens of 5.3.5 and not that lens: every form is in brackets.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.15.1
name: words-are-rows-beside-the-nodes
kind: decision
is: An entry's word is a row, a word and the entry it names, in a list a bound entry answers (lens/words.ck). No node holds its own word, so renaming changes no id and a second lexicon can name the same entries otherwise. Entries below 128 are named by the bootstrap's pages; from 128 up by modules written in form.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.2 5.13.3 5.1.5
src: 2.30.14 2.30.15
conf: mid
limit: No row yet says which numbers a module may take; which a module has is a convention of the lexicon.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.15.2
name: showing-is-total-and-reading-inverts-it
kind: decision
is: The lens shows every node: a word or #N for an entry, a number for a short int or nat, quotes for a plain text, brackets with hex for every other leaf, a name or $k for an argument, braces for a recipe, and brackets around a blueprint and its children for any other composite. Reading takes all of these and gives back the node that was shown. A node written twice is one node, so sharing needs no mark. Text it cannot read is a fault whose reason is a word and the place.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.3 5.3.8 5.3.4 5.14.31
src: 2.30.14 2.30.15
conf: mid
why: The notation is the one the observer already prints, so what is watched and what is written look the same.
flip: A part a let names (5.15.5) is written once. Sharing that no let names still shows in full each time it appears.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.15.3
name: the-lens-reads-its-own-definition
kind: observation
is: Observed on 2026-11-25. The lens shows its own stored node, through its own rows and names, as 984 lines and reads them back to the file it is stored as, byte for byte in both lines. Names were first read by a second lens written in the first; its 85 definitions were then named with no byte of its node changing. ground/lens-round-trip.ck shows, reads and stores again each of the 101 files of nodes here and names none that differs, in 0.8 s. Fourteen malformed texts each faulted with a word and a place.
status: observed
src: 2.30.14 2.30.15 2.30.7
limit: The list of files is a node of its own, ground/files.ck, so a new file is not covered until it is added there. One machine.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.15.4
name: a-row-is-two-shapes-run-both-ways
kind: decision
is: A row is a pair of nodes with holes: the surface a person writes and the core it stands for. One matcher written in form runs it both ways. Reading lowers surface to core, showing raises core to surface; the first row that fits is used, what is under quote is left alone, and a hole named rest holds the remaining children. Rows are a list a bound entry answers (lens/rows.ck), written in the lens itself: if, first, when, not and tuple. Adding a row adds both its reading and its showing.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.2 5.1.5 5.15.2 5.14.30
src: 2.30.14 2.30.15
conf: mid
why: 5.3.2 asks for rows read forwards and run backwards with no printer written by hand. Between two shapes of nodes that is matching and substitution, and it leaves text to the words and leaves beneath. A surface word is an entry of a higher namespace that lowers into the core (5.1.5).
flip: A pattern over text, as an infix operator or a host language needs, is not a pair of node shapes; that part of 5.3.2 is not built.
limit: A hole may stand once on each side. Every row is tried at every node.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.15.5
name: names-lie-beside-the-node-they-name
kind: decision
is: An argument is a number in the node and a name beside it. The names of a recipe's arguments and the name a let gives are a row keyed by that node's content id, kept on a second line of the node's file, which the bootstrap does not read. Text names arguments in braces, {a b | body}, and a let's part before its value; the reader turns a name into its number, and the printer shows the name where reading it back gives that number. Naming changes no node and no id.
status: inferred
from: 5.2.4 5.2.1 5.1.1 5.15.1 5.14.43
src: 2.30.14 2.30.15 2.30.3
conf: mid
why: 5.2.4 keeps names as rows keyed by node and lens and bound variables by position. The id is the one identity a row can carry between processes (5.2.1). Keeping the rows in the node's own file means they are written whenever the node is and cannot go stale.
flip: Two recipes that are one node share one row, so they cannot be named differently. A second lens with other names for the same node would need its own rows; the line holds one lens's.
limit: A recipe inside another sees none of the outer names, as a recipe captures nothing. In a recipe with no names the first let name takes argument 0.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.15.6
name: a-sign-stands-between-two
kind: decision
is: An operation of two children that has a sign is written with the sign between them, in brackets of its own: (n < 2) is (lt n 2). The signs are rows of data the lens holds, a sign and its entry, for add, sub, mul, div, mod, lt and eq. Reading takes the form when the second thing in a bracket is a sign; showing uses it for every such node. A sign between more than two is refused, so there is no precedence to learn and none to get wrong.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.2 5.3.5 5.15.2 5.15.4
src: 2.30.14 2.30.15
conf: mid
why: 5.3.2 names an operator table among a lens's rows. Arithmetic and tests are most of what is read in a definition, and written in front they hide what is compared with what.
flip: A reader used to precedence must write every bracket. A pattern over text, which a host language's lens needs, is still not built.
limit: Seven signs. A name may not be a sign.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.15.7
name: an-inner-recipe-sees-the-outer-names
kind: decision
is: A recipe written in braces inside another is read with the outer names in sight. If it uses one, it is the inner recipe closed over all the outer arguments (5.14.52), which come first among its own; if it uses none, it is a plain recipe, read by itself. A name of its own that repeats an outer one, an outer argument without a name, or an argument written by number inside it makes it plain. Showing gives back the braces where reading them would give the same node.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.5 5.2.4 5.15.5 5.14.52
src: 2.30.14 2.30.15
conf: mid
why: 5.3.5 asks for lambdas. Without this an inner recipe had to be passed everything by hand, or the outer text repeated.
flip: All outer arguments are closed over, not only those used. The inner text is read twice when it turns out to use none.
limit: Every file here reads back to itself with it, in one process and across two.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.15.8
name: a-shape-is-an-entry-and-its-constructor
kind: decision
is: A shape is an entry that is the blueprint of its instances, bound to a recipe that joins its arguments under it; the names of that recipe's arguments are the names of the shape's parts. A name may say the shape of what it names (mine:emit.row), and then mine.ints is read as the child at that part's place and shown back so. The part is found when the text is read: the stored node holds a number, the running program pays nothing, and the name with its shape lies beside the node like any other name.
status: inferred
from: 5.1.1 5.1.3 5.2.4 5.15.5 5.5.3
src: 2.30.14 2.30.15
conf: mid
why: 5.1.1 allows a node nothing but a blueprint and children, so a record is a node under its shape and a part is a child. What was missing was only the name of the part, and names already lie beside nodes.
flip: Nothing checks that a value has the shape its name says. A part is named only from a name, not from an expression.
limit: The shape must have been read before its parts are named, in the same reading, or its parts named in the lexicon's row for the entry, which is how let, recipe and the interfaces of one and of two parts are named.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.15.9
name: an-if-of-several-conditions
kind: decision
is: (if c a d b e) is (if c a (if d b e)), for any number of conditions. Reading nests, showing writes an if whose last part is an if in a row, with each condition above what it gives. No node changes: every stored definition that was a ladder of ifs is shown flat.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.5 5.15.4
src: 2.30.14 2.30.15
conf: mid
why: The emitter's largest definition was shown 80 columns deep. Its cases are alternatives of one rank, and the text should say so.
flip: An if written out nested is shown flat when read back.
at: 2026-11-26
